APX.jp = {"country":{"name":"Japan","tagline":"A thousand years of the Koshu grape, a rainy climate tamed by pergolas, and a new generation of growers from Yamanashi to Hokkaido.","classification":"Japan's wine GIs are designated by the National Tax Agency (NTA), which regulates alcohol. Five wine GIs exist, each covering a whole prefecture: Yamanashi (2013), Hokkaido (2018), and Yamagata, Osaka and Nagano (2021). Each GI has production standards: grapes grown entirely in the prefecture, a list of approved varieties (including vinifera, Koshu, Muscat Bailey A and Yamabudo hybrids in some GIs), minimum sugar levels at harvest, limits on chaptalisation and acidification, and in some cases a tasting panel. Yamanashi's standards, for example, require approved varieties and set minimum natural sugar levels for Koshu and Muscat Bailey A.\n\nSeparately, labelling rules in force since October 2018 define 'Japan Wine' (nihon wine) as wine made in Japan from 100 percent Japanese grapes, and allow a place, variety or vintage on the label only if at least 85 percent of the wine meets it. 'Domestically made wine' (kokunai seizo wine) can contain imported juice or concentrate and must say so. This distinction matters because the majority of wine bottled in Japan is still made from imported material."},"regions":[{"name":"Yamanashi","slug":"yamanashi","tagline":"The Kofu basin under Mount Fuji, home of Koshu and of a third of Japan's wineries.","ids":["jp-yamanashi"]},{"name":"Nagano","slug":"nagano","tagline":"Japan's high, dry wine valleys, where Merlot found a home on the Kikyogahara plateau.","ids":["jp-nagano"]},{"name":"Hokkaido","slug":"hokkaido","tagline":"Snowy, cool and largely free of typhoons: Japan's new frontier for Pinot Noir.","ids":["jp-hokkaido"]},{"name":"Yamagata","slug":"yamagata","tagline":"Fruit country in Tohoku with a century-old wine tradition.","ids":["jp-yamagata"]},{"name":"Osaka","slug":"osaka","tagline":"Hillside Delaware vineyards on the edge of a megacity, once Japan's largest grape region.","ids":["jp-osaka"]}],"items":{"jp-hokkaido":{"name":"Hokkaido","cat":"GI","subcat":"NTA wine GI (prefecture)","region":"Hokkaido","created":"28 June 2018","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Kerner, Pinot Noir, Zweigelt, Müller-Thurgau, Chardonnay, Yamabudo","summary":"Japan's second wine GI, designated in 2018, covering the whole of Hokkaido. The island's cool climate, with almost no rainy season and few typhoons, suits German and Burgundian varieties. Yoichi, west of Otaru, is the leading area: Takahiko Soga, who trained at Coco Farm in Tochigi, set up there in 2010, and Domaine Takahiko's Nana-Tsu-Mori Pinot Noir, made with whole bunches and little intervention, became the most sought-after Japanese red, inspiring dozens of new growers. In 2017 the Burgundy family estate Domaine de Montille bought land near Hakodate to make Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.","terroir":"Cool (around 1,000 to 1,300 growing degree days in the main areas), with heavy winter snow that insulates vines; volcanic and alluvial soils.","producers":["Domaine Takahiko","Domaine de Montille Hakodate","Hokkaido Wine (Otaru)","Tokachi Wine (Ikeda)","Takizawa","10R Winery","Yamazaki Winery","Domaine Atsushi Suzuki"],"nerd_note":"Ikeda town's Tokachi Wine, founded in 1963, was Japan's first municipally owned winery and still runs a 'wine castle' that ages wine in cellars.","rslug":"hokkaido"},"jp-nagano":{"name":"Nagano","cat":"GI","subcat":"NTA wine GI (prefecture)","region":"Nagano","created":"30 June 2021","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Merlot, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc, Concord, Niagara","summary":"A wine GI designated in 2021 for Nagano prefecture, whose high, dry valleys make it one of Japan's best regions for European varieties. The Kikyogahara plateau at Shiojiri, where Château Mercian persuaded growers to switch from Concord to Merlot in 1976, produces Japan's best-known Merlot. The Chikumagawa valley near Ueda and Tomi (Villa d'Est, Mercian's Mariko vineyard), Azumino and the Kiso valley have dozens of newer wineries.","terroir":"Altitudes of 500 to 900 m, the lowest rainfall in Japan, cold winters and large day-night temperature swings; volcanic and gravelly soils.","producers":["Château Mercian (Kikyogahara, Mariko)","Villa d'Est","Izutsu","Rue de Vin","Obuse Winery","Domaine Sogga","Manns Wines (Komoro)"],"nerd_note":"Obuse Winery's Domaine Sogga, run by Akihiko Soga (Takahiko's brother), was one of the first Japanese wineries to farm organically and make wine with wild yeast.","rslug":"nagano"},"jp-osaka":{"name":"Osaka","cat":"GI","subcat":"NTA wine GI (prefecture)","region":"Osaka","created":"30 June 2021","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Delaware, Muscat Bailey A, Koshu, Chardonnay","summary":"A wine GI designated in 2021 for Osaka prefecture, whose hillside vineyards around Kashiwara and Habikino were Japan's largest grape-growing area in the 1930s. Katashimo Winery, founded in 1914, is the oldest winery in western Japan and makes Delaware, Koshu and Muscat Bailey A.","terroir":"Warm, humid slopes of the Ikoma and Kongo ranges overlooking the city.","producers":["Katashimo Winery","Kashiwara Winery","Shimanouchi Fujimaru Winery"],"rslug":"osaka"},"jp-yamagata":{"name":"Yamagata","cat":"GI","subcat":"NTA wine GI (prefecture)","region":"Yamagata","created":"30 June 2021","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Delaware, Chardonnay, Merlot, Muscat Bailey A, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A wine GI designated in 2021 for Yamagata prefecture in northern Honshu, a fruit region whose about 15 wineries make wines from European and American varieties, notably sparkling and still Delaware. Takahata Winery and Sakai Winery (founded 1892, among the oldest in Japan) are leading producers.","terroir":"Inland basins with hot summers and snowy winters.","producers":["Takahata Winery","Sakai Winery","Tsukinoi Winery","Asahimachi Wine"],"rslug":"yamagata"},"jp-yamanashi":{"name":"Yamanashi","cat":"GI","subcat":"NTA wine GI (prefecture)","region":"Yamanashi","created":"16 July 2013","styles":["white","red","sparkling","orange"],"grapes":"Koshu, Muscat Bailey A, Merlot, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Japan's first wine Geographical Indication, designated by the National Tax Agency in 2013, covers all of Yamanashi prefecture. Its rules require 100 percent Yamanashi grapes from listed varieties (led by Koshu and Muscat Bailey A, with European vinifera), minimum natural sugar levels and limits on chaptalisation, and wines must pass a tasting panel. In practice it is the GI for Koshu: the pale, low-alcohol white grown mostly in the eastern Kofu basin around Katsunuma.\n\nYamanashi has about 80 wineries, from Château Mercian (whose roots go back to 1877) and Grace Wine (Chuo Budoshu, founded 1923, where Ayana Misawa makes acclaimed Koshu and sparkling) to small new growers. Its Koshu ranges from crisp and citrusy to sur lie, barrel-aged, sparkling and skin-contact styles; its Muscat Bailey A gives light, fruity reds; and Merlot and Cabernet from the drier slopes are increasingly serious.","terroir":"The Kofu basin is ringed by mountains (Mount Fuji to the south) that block some rain; summers are hot and humid. Vineyards occupy alluvial fans and slopes at 300 to 700 m, often on traditional high pergolas (tanazukuri). Akeno and Hokuto in the north-west are higher and sunnier.","producers":["Château Mercian","Grace Wine (Chuo Budoshu)","Lumière","Marufuji (Rubaiyat)","Haramo","Sadoya","Katsunuma Jozo","Kizan","Domaine Mie Ikeno"],"nerd_note":"Koshu DNA analysis (Goto-Yamamoto et al., 2015) found it is mostly vinifera with some ancestry from wild East Asian Vitis, including Vitis davidii, which may help it cope with humidity.","rslug":"yamanashi"}}};
